# .env.example  —  copy to .env, which .gitignore already excludes.
#
# SECRETS, plus the few settings that describe WHERE THIS PROCESS RUNS. Nothing else.
#
# (This file previously said "secrets only, there will never be a fifth entry". The
# relayer's exposure controls made that too narrow, so the rule is restated rather than
# quietly broken: a value belongs here if it is a secret, or if it describes this
# particular deployment. Network parameters still never do.)
#
# Network parameters — pool address, RPC hosts, chain ID, prover, explorer — are FACTS
# ABOUT A NETWORK, not secrets. They live in packages/protocol/src/constants.ts where
# they are version-controlled, reviewable and diffable. Putting an RPC URL or a pool
# address here instead would mean the value a judge can read in the repository is not
# provably the value the code ran against. Do not add one.
#
# THE DEPLOYER AND THE RELAYER MUST BE TWO SEPARATELY FUNDED WALLETS.
# This is not a convention, it is a test requirement. Task 8's identity_key probe proves
# that the pool derives a DIFFERENT per-user handle for different callers of the same
# helper contract. With one wallet playing both roles there are not two callers, so the
# probe cannot distinguish "scoped per user" from "constant", and it proves nothing.
# Fund both before deploying.

DEPLOYER_ADDRESS=0x...
DEPLOYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...
RELAYER_ADDRESS=0x...
RELAYER_PRIVATE_KEY=0x...


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Relayer exposure controls. All optional, and the defaults are the safe ones — but
# read this before putting the relayer behind anything.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# REQUIRED IF THIS SERVER IS REACHABLE THROUGH A PROXY. Not optional there.
#
# A shared secret, sent by the caller as the `x-relayer-auth` header. When set, every
# request must carry it; without it the server answers 401 and never touches the key.
#
# Why a proxy makes this mandatory: the browser posts to the same-origin relative path
# /api/submit, and the server accepts that path precisely so a proxy can forward it.
# The moment that rewrite exists, loopback has stopped being a boundary — and NOTHING
# warns you, because the off-host warning keys off RELAYER_HOST, which you never
# changed. Behind a proxy every internet client arrives without an Origin header, which
# is exactly the shape the other checks treat as a trusted same-process caller.
#
# Content-type is a CSRF control. It is not authentication. This is.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# RELAYER_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Browser origins allowed to post, comma-separated. Default (unset) is the safe one.
#
# Be clear about what this can do: it can REFUSE, and it cannot GRANT. A browser
# request carrying both an Origin and content-type: application/json is by definition
# cross-origin, so it is preflighted — and this server answers no CORS headers, so the
# request never arrives. Configuring an origin does not enable a browser caller; it
# only narrows which non-browser callers are refused. Do not reach for it as a way to
# let a web app in, and do not mistake it for RELAYER_AUTH_TOKEN.
#
# Blank entries are dropped, so an empty value and a stray comma cannot widen it.
# RELAYER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example

# Interface to bind. Defaults to 127.0.0.1, and empty is treated as unset.
#
# Setting this exposes a funded signer beyond this machine. Treat it as a gate that
# authentication and rate limiting must come BEFORE — the allowlist bounds what may be
# signed, never by whom or how often.
# RELAYER_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Port. Defaults to 8787.
# PORT=8787


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sponsorship budget. The relayer pays for what it signs, so these are the numbers
# that bound what a bad day costs. All optional; the defaults are deliberately small.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Sponsored submissions per visitor per UTC day. Defaults to 1.
#
# A visitor is a salted hash of the client IP, re-keyed every UTC day — the relayer sees
# IPs by design (shielding IP+intent from THIRD parties is the service), but the durable
# ledger it keeps is not a log of who visited. Behind a proxy every caller arrives with
# the proxy's address, so this collapses into the daily budget; that refuses too much
# rather than too little, and trusting x-forwarded-for instead would make it a suggestion.
# RELAYER_SPONSOR_PER_VISITOR=1

# Sponsored submissions across everyone per UTC day. Defaults to 20.
#
# At a 6 STRK pool fee, 20 is around 120 STRK plus gas — this is the day's exposure, not
# the balance the wallet has to hold at any moment. Raise it only alongside the operating
# rule in server.ts: keep this wallet on a small working balance and top it up, so a
# mistake costs a batch rather than a balance. A blank value means unset; a value that is
# not a positive integer fails at startup rather than quietly reverting to the default.
# RELAYER_SPONSOR_DAILY=20

# Where the SPONSORSHIP ledger lives. Defaults to .relayer/sponsorship.json in the repository
# root, created on first boot. The send cap has its own file — see RELAYER_SEND_STORE below.
#
# It survives restarts on purpose: a relayer that forgets what it spent has a budget per
# uptime, not a budget per day. It is ONE process's state — do not point two relayers at
# one file, they will clobber each other's counters and both believe they are under
# budget. A file that exists but does not parse is a hard startup failure, never a silent
# reset, because "the ledger was unreadable" and "spend the whole day again" must not be
# the same outcome.
# RELAYER_SPONSOR_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/sponsorship.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The cap on PLAIN submissions — the ones that pay their own way (story 1.16).
#
# A sponsored registration mints nothing, so the relayer's own STRK pays its fee and the
# budget above is a spending limit. A SEND folds a Withdraw naming this relayer into its own
# proven action chain, so the POOL FEE comes back out of the sender's notes.
#
# NOT "free" — the execution gas is still ours. A send reimburses the fee and nothing else, so
# every relayed send costs this wallet the gas for the transaction, every time. Much smaller
# than a whole sponsored fee; not zero. That is why sends are metered at all.
#
# The two are metered separately for two reasons beyond cost: a busy day of sends must not
# spend the free registrations this product exists to hand out, and a refused send must not be
# told that account creation is paused.
#
# This cap also bounds the rate at which an unreimbursed batch could drain the wallet, because
# the relayer cannot check for the leg — apply_actions calldata is deliberately uninspected
# (allowlist.ts). What bounds the LOSS is the wallet balance and the funding monitor, which
# refuses to sign below two live fees whatever these numbers say.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Relayed sends per visitor per UTC day. Defaults to 3 — more than the one sponsored
# registration a visitor gets, because a send costs gas rather than a whole fee, and a session
# that sends once usually sends again. Same visitor definition and same salt as the
# sponsorship budget, so both gates bucket one address the same way.
# RELAYER_SEND_PER_VISITOR=3

# Relayed sends across everyone per UTC day. Defaults to 20 — the same daily ceiling as the
# sponsorship budget, so neither gate is the surprising one.
# RELAYER_SEND_DAILY=20

# Where the send cap's ledger lives. Defaults to .relayer/send-budget.json in the
# repository root, created on first boot.
#
# A SEPARATE FILE from the sponsorship ledger, deliberately. One file holding both would
# make a reset of either a reset of both, so an operator clearing a stuck send counter
# would silently hand out a fresh day of free registrations. Everything the sponsorship
# store's note says about durability, single-process ownership and unparseable files
# applies here too.
# RELAYER_SEND_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/send-budget.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Invites (story 1.14 / FR-014). OFF unless RELAYER_INVITE_ALLOWANCE is set.
#
# An invite is the fix for this app's most common hard error — the pool rejects transfers to an
# unregistered key — not a growth feature. A registered user mints a six-character code, shares
# a link, and the stranger who opens it gets one sponsored registration.
#
# THE FEATURE HAS A MASTER SWITCH RATHER THAN A DEFAULT, and that is deliberate: there is no
# defensible default for how many free registrations one address may hand out to strangers. That
# is a giveaway policy, not a tuning number. Setting any OTHER RELAYER_INVITE_* variable while
# RELAYER_INVITE_ALLOWANCE is unset FAILS AT STARTUP — an operator who configured a TTL has
# plainly decided to run invites, and quietly not having the feature because they missed the one
# switch is the same silent misconfiguration this file guards against everywhere else.
#
# WHAT A BURNED CODE BUYS IS A WAIVER, NOT A BYPASS. It waives the PER-VISITOR sponsorship cap
# and nothing else. That cap is a fairness limit — one free account per address per day — and it
# is the one that misfires on a shared address: a stranger on a NAT-shared mobile IP whose cap
# other strangers already spent is exactly who an invite is for. The GLOBAL DAILY BUDGET above
# still applies, unconditionally, and when it is spent an invited visitor gets the same honest
# degrade into pay-your-own-way as everyone else. The budget is the relayer's solvency floor;
# an invite must never become a promise the wallet cannot keep.
#
# Sponsorship covers REGISTRATION ONLY, never balance. A farmed account can receive and cannot
# send, which is worth zero — that is the second of the five abuse layers, and it is why a
# guessed code is not worth much even if someone gets one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Codes one inviter may mint per rolling window. UNSET MEANS INVITES ARE OFF ENTIRELY: the three
# invite routes answer 404 and a code presented on /submit is refused rather than ignored.
#
# Small, because every one of these is a pool fee this relayer pays. 3 is adapted from Bluesky's
# invite allowance rather than derived from anything — a defensible starting point, not a
# measurement. Raise it alongside RELAYER_SPONSOR_DAILY, never past it — and that is ENFORCED at
# startup, not merely advised: an allowance above the daily budget mints invites that can only
# ever degrade into pay-your-own-way, so the server refuses the combination. Ceiling 1,000.
#
# THE NAT CAVEAT CUTS THIS WAY TOO. The waiver below exists because the per-visitor cap misfires
# on a shared address — and the mint allowance is keyed on an address hash just the same, so
# everyone behind one office NAT or CGNAT shares ONE allowance of 3. That refuses too much
# rather than too little, which is the direction every gate here fails in; a busy shared network
# that needs more mints is a reason to raise this number, not to re-key the ledger.
# RELAYER_INVITE_ALLOWANCE=3

# The rolling window the allowance is measured over, in hours. Defaults to 24.
#
# THE INVITER HASH IS NOT DAY-SALTED, unlike the visitor id the budgets use, and this window is
# why. The budget gates hash H(salt|utcDay|ip) so yesterday's ids cannot be matched to today's
# and so the caps reset at 00:00 UTC — which is the promise their notice makes. A rolling 24-hour
# window keyed on that same id would silently reset every inviter's allowance at midnight, so
# "1 more in 19h" would be a promise the ledger forgets on the way there. The invite ledger
# therefore keys mints on H(salt|ip), with the same salt and no day. The cost is stated plainly:
# those ids are stable for as long as the salt is, so rotating the ledger file rotates them.
# RELAYER_INVITE_WINDOW_HOURS=24

# How long a minted code stays claimable, in hours. Defaults to 72.
#
# Long enough that a link sent in the evening still works the next day; short enough that the
# live population of bearer codes stays small, which is one of the four things that make six
# characters sufficient. Expiry gates CLAIMING only — once a code is claimed it can still pay for
# the registration it was claimed for, because refusing afterwards would strand a real invitee in
# the middle of the ceremony for reading carefully.
# RELAYER_INVITE_TTL_HOURS=72

# Claim attempts one visitor may make per UTC day, successful or not. Defaults to 10.
#
# THIS IS WHAT MAKES A SIX-CHARACTER BEARER CODE SAFE, so it is not a knob to raise casually.
# The arithmetic, written down once: 32^6 is about 1.07e9 codes; the live population is at most
# the allowance times the number of inviters, and it expires; this caps how fast anyone can
# guess; and the prize for guessing one is a single sponsored registration for an account that
# can receive but not send. Remove any one of those four and the code length has to be revisited.
#
# It also meters MISSES on the status route and refusals on /submit's invite vet, which would
# otherwise be free, unlimited oracles for "is this code live and unclaimed" — the exact question
# that would walk around this cap. Every code-bearing route is cap-first: a visitor past the cap
# is refused before the code is even looked up, hits included, so the shape of a refusal stops
# distinguishing live codes from dead ones. A sender polling their own real code is never
# charged. Ceiling 10,000, because a value that unmakes the safety argument above should have to
# be typed somewhere more deliberate than an env file.
# RELAYER_INVITE_CLAIM_ATTEMPTS=10

# Mints per rolling 24 hours across EVERY inviter together. Defaults to 50. Ceiling 100,000.
#
# The per-inviter allowance is a fairness bound; this is a solvency-shaped one, like the daily
# budgets. Addresses are cheap — a rotating IPv6 /64 mints a fresh inviter hash per request — so
# without a global ceiling the "allowance" is only a speed limit per identity an attacker can
# mint for free, the ledger file grows without bound, and every live code slightly weakens the
# 32^6 guessing arithmetic. Refusals under this cap carry the same clocked, never-a-locked-door
# shape as the allowance refusal; `reason` distinguishes them for ops.
# RELAYER_INVITE_MINT_DAILY=50

# Where the invite ledger lives. Defaults to .relayer/invites.json in the repository root,
# created on first boot.
#
# A THIRD FILE, for the reason the send cap needed a second one. One file holding all three would
# make a reset of any of them a reset of all — and the reset that must never happen by accident
# is the one that un-burns every claimed code, because a burn that an unrelated maintenance
# action can undo is not a burn. Everything the sponsorship store's note says about durability,
# single-process ownership and unparseable files applies here too, and the consequence of a
# silent reset is worse: an unreadable invite ledger read as empty re-opens every allowance and
# un-burns every code at once.
#
# SALT ROTATION IS COUPLED, ONE WAY. This ledger shares the sponsorship store's salt, and its
# inviter hashes are day-independent, so deleting or rotating the SPONSORSHIP store (which mints
# the shared salt) makes an established invite ledger a STARTUP ERROR rather than a silent
# re-key — adopting a new salt would reset every rolling allowance and orphan every attempt
# count with nothing in any log. The error says what to do: if the reset is intended, move or
# delete this file deliberately and restart.
# RELAYER_INVITE_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/invites.json

# Overrides the salt used to hash visitor IPs, for BOTH ledgers — the sponsorship store mints
# it and the send store is opened with the same one, so a visitor buckets identically in each.
# Leave it unset: that is the better default, because the sponsorship store mints its own on
# first boot, nobody has to hold it, and it dies with the file. Set it only to rotate
# deliberately: a new salt re-keys every visitor id, so the current day's per-visitor counters
# — in both ledgers — stop matching the visitors they counted.
#
# Must be at least 32 hexadecimal characters, checked at startup. A short salt is worse
# than no salt: it reads in this file as though privacy were configured while raising the
# cost of a brute-force by nothing. Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Be clear about what the hashing buys. The ledger holds no IP, so reading a counter is not
# reading a visitor, and the UTC day is mixed in so yesterday's ids cannot be matched to
# today's. It does NOT make the file one-way: the salt is stored beside the hashes it
# produced, so anyone holding the file can brute-force the whole IPv4 space against it.
# Treat the ledger as sensitive, not as anonymised.
# RELAYER_VISITOR_SALT=

# Per-IP daily ceiling on POST /api/quote. Defaults to 100.
#
# Its own counter, never the sponsorship budget — a quote costs egress, a submission costs
# STRK, and charging quotes against the budget would let anyone burn a visitor's sponsored
# registration by asking for prices. The counter is in memory and resets on restart, which
# is fine: the cap exists so the proxy cannot become someone's free anonymity service, not
# to protect a balance.
# RELAYER_QUOTE_DAILY_PER_VISITOR=100

# Quote requests across everyone per UTC day. Defaults to 1000.
#
# The per-visitor cap bounds one address; it does not bound the bill. Anyone with a rotating
# IPv6 /64 mints a fresh visitor id per request, so without a ceiling across everyone the
# per-visitor number is only a speed limit per identity they can create for free. Same
# reasoning as the sponsorship daily budget, and the same in-memory counter.
# RELAYER_QUOTE_DAILY_GLOBAL=1000

# Where a funding page goes. Unset means the log, under a greppable "relayer: OPS"
# prefix. Set it and each page is also POSTed as {"text": "..."} to this URL.
#
# The relayer pays every fee it signs out of its own STRK balance. When that balance runs
# out, apply_actions starts reverting on the fee — a revert that looks like our bug. Two
# thresholds guard it, both multiples of the LIVE fee so they follow a fee change:
#
#   below 5 live fees   ops is paged; the relayer keeps working normally
#   below 2 live fees   the relayer refuses to sign and the submit path answers 503
#
# At today's 6 STRK fee that is a page under 30 STRK and refusal under 12 STRK. Refusal is
# set low on purpose: it has to mean "very nearly unable to pay the next submission", so
# that the operating rule in server.ts — keep this wallet on a small working balance — does
# not leave the relayer permanently below its own floor. The two are separate numbers
# rather than one scaled twice, so the page still arrives with room to act on it.
#
# The user only ever sees the ordinary relayer-down state, never a number.
# RELAYER_OPS_WEBHOOK=https://hooks.example/services/...

# How often to re-read the relayer's STRK balance, in milliseconds. Defaults to 300000
# (5 minutes). There is always one read at startup, before the socket opens, so the first
# request is answered from a measurement rather than from "unknown".
#
# 0 is a real setting here: keep the startup check, never poll.
# RELAYER_FUNDING_INTERVAL_MS=300000

# Where the SESSION SCRIPTS reach a relayer over HTTP. Describes where THIS process
# runs — the file's own rule for belonging here — and nothing else reads it: the
# browser resolves /api/submit against its own origin, so this is only for headless
# drivers like scripts/bank-sponsored-registration.ts. Defaults to the loopback server
# `npx tsx packages/relayer/src/server.ts` listens on.
# RELAYER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/submit

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The starter drip (M8, drip-first: the ONE subsidy). OFF by default — the faucet gives
# away PRINCIPAL, on mainnet, to anyone who asks; an operator turns it on deliberately,
# and a fresh clone of this repository cannot start handing out its wallet because
# somebody forgot a cap.
#
# THE DRIP STAKES THE WHOLE JOURNEY: it fires FIRST (a plain transfer lands on a
# counterfactual address), the account deploys itself from it, registration is signed
# and PAID BY THE USER from the drip (`collect_fee` pulls from whoever submits), and
# what remains is the starter. Sponsored registration demotes to the fallback for when
# this faucet is off or dry — never a locked door, never a second subsidy.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The master switch. Exactly the string "on" enables /api/faucet; anything else is off
# and the route answers 404.
# RELAYER_FAUCET=on

# The drip, in wei. Default 10 STRK — account-deploy gas + the ~6 STRK pool fee +
# headroom + starter. The exact number is the operator's call at flip-on.
# RELAYER_FAUCET_DRIP_WEI=10000000000000000000

# Drips per visitor per UTC day (default 1) and across everyone (default 2). daily ×
# drip IS the day's acquisition budget in STRK — at the defaults, ~20 STRK buys two
# complete new users a day.
# RELAYER_FAUCET_PER_VISITOR=1
# RELAYER_FAUCET_DAILY=2

# Where the faucet ledger lives. A FOURTH file — a busy day of drips must not spend the
# free registrations. On Fly this must point at the volume or every deploy resets
# "once per address, ever".
# RELAYER_FAUCET_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/faucet.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The chain feed (M1) — one poller for every open tab, /api/chain/stream.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Set to "off" to disable the feed entirely; the browser falls back to polling.
# RELAYER_CHAIN_FEED=off

# Where the price-history JSONL lives. Not a ledger — losing it costs a chart its past,
# nobody a cap — but the volume is where "the past this process witnessed" belongs.
# RELAYER_CHAIN_FEED_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/chain-feed.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The logo studio (M3) — /api/logo/pin and /api/logo/generate. Each lane exists exactly
# when its key does (no key, no route), and neither credential ever reaches a browser.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pinata JWT (uploads.pinata.cloud/v3/files). The pin lane.
# RELAYER_PINATA_JWT=

# Gemini API key (the OpenAI-compat images endpoint). The generate lane. The model rides
# its own variable so a better image model is a config change, not a deploy.
# RELAYER_GEMINI_KEY=
# RELAYER_GEMINI_IMAGE_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-image

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# X connect (M5). The OAuth legs are VERCEL functions (api/x/*) — cookies and redirects
# do not survive the forwarder — so X_CLIENT_ID, X_CLIENT_SECRET and X_SESSION_SECRET
# belong in the VERCEL environment. They appear here only so a local `vercel dev` run
# and this file agree about the names. RELAYER_XBIND_SECRET is the one that must exist
# in BOTH places: the Vercel function presents it and this relayer demands it on
# /directory/x-bind — the second lock that keeps a browser from minting attestations.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# X_CLIENT_ID=
# X_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
# X_SESSION_SECRET=
# RELAYER_XBIND_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Teller (M7) — signer five, the governance key custodian.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Where the per-proposal tally secrets live. Its own file, the fourth-ledger discipline;
# on Fly it must be on the volume. Losing it before a permanently-private close makes
# that vote untalliable — void_proposal is the backstop, never the plan.
# RELAYER_TELLER_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/teller.json

# The block the Teller scans BallotCast events from. Set it to the blockNumber
# deploy-governance.ts prints; without it the scan starts at 0, which public RPCs refuse.
# RELAYER_GOVERNANCE_FROM_BLOCK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Groundskeeper — standing markets, so the board is never empty.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# OFF by default because every sweep can spend real STRK: one seed per market it opens, plus a
# one-time self-registration (the pool fee, its own gas) and a shielding deposit on first boot.
# The relayer account becomes a pool participant — op_create lives inside privacy_invoke, so a
# market opens with liquidity or not at all. Pacing is the budget control: one creation per
# sweep, one sweep every three minutes, and only for a pair with no open market.
# RELAYER_GROUNDSKEEPER=on

# The seed each standing market opens with, in wei. Default 2 STRK.
# RELAYER_GROUNDSKEEPER_SEED_WEI=2000000000000000000

# The seeder secrets — each market's bearer claim on its own pot. MONEY: a lost secret is a
# seed nobody can reclaim. On Fly this must be on the volume.
# RELAYER_GROUNDSKEEPER_STORE=/var/lib/passbook/groundskeeper.json
